<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">12873814</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2004</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>07</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2023</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>15</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">0168-8278</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>39</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2003</Year><Month>Aug</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of hepatology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Hepatol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Spironolactone alone or in combination with furosemide in the treatment of moderate ascites in nonazotemic cirrhosis. A randomized comparative study of efficacy and safety.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>187</StartPage><EndPage>192</EndPage><MedlinePgn>187-92</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND/AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The most rational treatment of moderate ascites is spironolactone alone or in combination with furosemide. However, it is unknown which of these two treatment schedules is preferable.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred nonazotemic cirrhotic patients with moderate ascites were randomly assigned to be treated with spironolactone and furosemide (Group 1: 50 patients) or with spironolactone alone (Group 2: 50 patients). If no response was obtained, the doses of diuretics were increased up to 400 mg/day of spironolactone and 160 mg/day of furosemide. In patients of group 2 not responding to 400 mg/day of spironolactone, furosemide was added. In cases with an excessive response, the dosage of diuretics was reduced.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The response rate (98% in Group 1 vs. 94% in Group 2), the rapidity of ascites mobilization and the incidence of complications induced by diuretic therapy was similar in both groups. The need to reduce the diuretic dosage was significantly higher in Group 1 than Group 2 (68% vs. 34%; P=0.002).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the treatment of moderate ascites, spironolactone alone seems to be as safe and effective as spironolactone associated with furosemide. Since spironolactone alone requires less dose adjustment, it would be more suitable for treating ascites on an outpatient basis.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Santos</LastName><ForeName>Justiniano</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Gastroenterology, Hospital Universitari Germans Tri;as i Pujol, Carretera del Canyet, s/n, 08916, Badalona, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Planas</LastName><ForeName>Ramon</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pardo</LastName><ForeName>Albert</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dur&#xe1;ndez</LastName><ForeName>Rosa</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Cabr&#xe9;</LastName><ForeName>Eduard</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Morillas</LastName><ForeName>Rosa Maria</ForeName><Initials>RM</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Granada</LastName><ForeName>Maria Luisa</ForeName><Initials>ML</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jim&#xe9;nez</LastName><ForeName>Jos&#xe9; Angel</ForeName><Initials>JA</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Quintero</LastName><ForeName>Enrique</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gassull</LastName><ForeName>Miquel Angel</ForeName><Initials>MA</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016430">Clinical Trial</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D003160">Comparative Study</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</Publicatio